<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Adaptive icon for API 26+ (Android 8.0).

    The launcher's mask is a system-drawn shape that varies by OEM and by the
    user's chosen icon shape, so neither layer carries a rounded corner or a
    shadow — those are the launcher's job. The background is therefore a flat
    fill and all the artwork lives in the foreground (**D-66**, **D-67**).

    The foreground's grid is inset further than the legacy icon's (see the
    GridFraction arithmetic in tool/generate_assets.ps1) because the launcher
    may crop the outer 18dp of the 108dp canvas, and because this layer has no
    background of its own to bleed into the cropped ring.

    There is deliberately no <monochrome> layer. Themed-icon support wants a
    single-colour silhouette, and this artwork is a four-colour grid whose
    meaning comes from the gray/orange split. Pointing <monochrome> at the
    colour foreground would just have the system tint the whole bitmap one
    colour, producing four indistinct blobs — an icon that looks broken on
    exactly the themed-icon surfaces the layer exists for. Omitting the layer
    means Android falls back to the colour adaptive icon, which is correct. If
    a monochrome silhouette is ever wanted it needs its own drawn asset.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
</adaptive-icon>
